all they had was  two days warning seven villages in  nagorno-karabakh  suddenly added to the first lot of  territory that passed into azerbaijani  hands this friday  these are new villages built by  armenians displaced in the first war in  the early 1990s  now forced to move again burn the homes  they've built  rather than leave them to azerbaijan  pack up their things and even dig up  what's most precious to them  the despair here is unbearable  the family we meet on a windswept farm  robbed of everything they care about  why did they take our children and kill  them this woman says  i don't even know where my dead child is  to bring and bury  and then the misery of her son  my brother is dead my relative is dead  why should i  stay he says it would only take one  knife here  and that's it  in the village of noor maraga we meet  only men  the women and children fled at the start  of this six week war  in soviet times angelique bogosian lived  in azerbaijan  they were his friends but not anymore  armenia is trying to prevent that  sending busloads of refugees to the  region's capitals to panacurt  trying to pack its diminishing lands  with people  but the homeland they call atsach is  much smaller now  and armenia has lost most of the seven  azerbaijani districts around it which  had served as a buffer zone  kalbajar is one of those the deadline to  leave was extended last week  but when we drive through it's deserted  already in these contested hillsides  history has a habit of repeating itself  it's armenians who've abandoned these  villages now  but at the end of the last war this was  an azerbaijani area  it was the azerbaijanis who fled on mass  from the armenians who had won that war  the petrol station here is vandalized  and there's a message for the newcomers  if and when they come  keorgia milian keeps guard and directs  the traffic  he lives across the mountains in  nagorno-karabakh and he swears he'll  never leave  him  by  the reminders of past wars are  inescapable here as is the awful feeling  of more to come  [Applause]  dynamically sky news in nagorno-karabakh
 

